//? Title: Fit a logistic model under complete separation
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Estimate a finite effect under complete separation

Six trials in group A all fail, and five trials in group B all
succeed. An ordinary logistic likelihood can increase indefinitely
in the direction of a larger group difference. Firth's adjustment
adds half the log determinant of the Fisher information to the
log likelihood, giving a finite fitted effect and probabilities for
both groups.

The design has an intercept and an indicator for group B.
The success counts are supplied directly with the trial counts.

```{cpp, id=firth-separated-groups, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/firth_logistic.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
    using datamunge::linalg::DenseMatrix;
    using datamunge::stats::ProfileEndpointStatus;
    const DenseMatrix<double> design(2, 2, {1, 0, 1, 1});
    const auto fit = datamunge::stats::firth_logistic(design, {0, 5}, {.trials = {6, 5}});
    const auto probability = fit.predict(design);
    std::println("| Group | Observed successes | Fitted success probability |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| A | 0 / 6 | {:.4f} |", probability[0]);
    std::println("| B | 5 / 5 | {:.4f} |", probability[1]);

    const auto& effect = fit.intervals[1];
    if (effect.lower.status != ProfileEndpointStatus::Crossing ||
        effect.upper.status != ProfileEndpointStatus::Crossing)
        throw std::runtime_error("Expected two penalized likelihood-ratio limits");
    std::println("\n| Group B versus A | Estimate | 95% penalized profile interval |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| Log odds ratio | {:.3f} | [{:.3f}, {:.3f}] |",
                 fit.coefficients[1], effect.lower.value, effect.upper.value);
    std::println("| Odds ratio | {:.1f} | [{:.2f}, {:.0f}] |", std::exp(fit.coefficients[1]),
                 std::exp(effect.lower.value), std::exp(effect.upper.value));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Group | Observed successes | Fitted success probability |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| A | 0 / 6 | 0.0714 |
| B | 5 / 5 | 0.9167 |

| Group B versus A | Estimate | 95% penalized profile interval |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Log odds ratio | 4.963 | [1.712, 10.767] |
| Odds ratio | 143.0 | [5.54, 47439] |
// result_end

The interval fixes the group coefficient at each candidate value and
refits the intercept while retaining the full model's information
penalty. Exponentiating the two log-odds limits gives the interval for
the odds ratio. Its width reflects the limited number of trials.

[Firth (1993)|https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/80.1.27] ·
[Heinze and Schemper (2002)|https://doi.org/10.1002/sim.1047]
